<mujoco>
  <extension>
    <plugin plugin="mujoco.elasticity.cable"/>
  </extension>
  <worldbody>
    <!-- EVA/EPE pool noodle cut to 1.2 m: 65 mm OD, 30 mm bore, 94 g, foam
         E ~ 0.8 MPa, nu ~ 0.3. MuJoCo's cable composite is a chain of solid
         capsules, so every number here is the solid-capsule equivalent of that
         hollow section, calibrated rather than guessed:
           density  gives a 94 g chain. It depends on count, because
                    consecutive capsule end caps overlap and double-count mass.
           bend     makes a 1.2 m cantilever of this chain droop 0.305 m, which
                    is what E = 0.8 MPa predicts for the real section. (The old
                    bend="1e5" drooped 1.018 m - that is a rope, not a noodle.)
           twist    = bend / (2 * (1 + nu)).

         count must satisfy  size/(count-1) >= 2*size_of_geom.  Capsules i and
         i+2 are born interpenetrating whenever the chain pitch falls below the
         capsule diameter, and the composite only excludes ADJACENT pairs. The
         old count="25" gave a 50 mm pitch against a 60 mm diameter, so every
         i/i+2 pair sat 10 mm inside its neighbour for the whole run: 44
         permanent contacts fighting the elasticity plugin, which is what made
         the rod itself visibly misbehave. At count="13" the pitch is 100 mm.
         The coarser chain also drops the stiffest bending mode from 240 Hz to
         85 Hz (omega*dt 1.51 -> 0.54, explicit limit ~2), which matters because
         mujoco_ros_control overrides opt.timestep to 1 ms and the step cannot
         be shortened from this file. -->
    <composite prefix="noodle/" type="cable" curve="s" count="13 1 1"
               size="1.2" offset="-0.6 0 0.2" initial="free">
      <plugin plugin="mujoco.elasticity.cable">
        <config key="twist" value="2.0e5"/>
        <config key="bend" value="5.2e5"/>
      </plugin>
      <!-- Foam is strongly dissipative (tan_delta ~ 0.1). These dampers act in
           series, so the whole-noodle value is damping/(count-1); 0.05 here is
           ~6% on the rod's torsional mode. Rescale it with count. -->
      <joint kind="main" damping="0.05"/>
      <!-- Bit 30 marks the connector. conaffinity="1" collides with the floor
           but not with itself; self-collision may be re-enabled with
           conaffinity="1073741825" as long as the count rule above holds. -->
      <geom type="capsule" size="0.0325" density="16.47" rgba="0.9 0.5 0.1 1"
            contype="1073741824" conaffinity="1"/>
    </composite>
  </worldbody>
  <equality>
    <!-- Clamped ends, matching the real mount. A weld transmits moment, which
         couples both drones' attitude loops, and a DEFAULT weld does it through
         an infinitely rigid clamp: the pair then diverges in symmetric roll
         within ~2 s of leaving the ground. torquescale="1" is the culprit -
         its units are METRES, so the default is a 1 m rotational lever on a
         ~0.3 m airframe. It never flies, at any solref.

         These values come from a 4x4 sweep of torquescale x solref timeconst
         (16 cells + repeats). The measured stability map was:

               ts \ tc    0.02   0.03   0.04   0.08
                  0.05     X      X      X      OK
                  0.1      X      OK     OK     OK
                  0.3      X      X      X      OK
                  1.0      X      X      X      X

         Read it before retuning, because it is NOT monotonic: ts=0.05 is
         *less* stable than ts=0.1, so a softer clamp is not a safer clamp.
         (0.1, 0.08) is the only cell with all its neighbours stable, which is
         why it is the default here; it is 4/4 reproducible. (0.1, 0.04) also
         flies but sits on a knife edge - both its ts neighbours fail.

         Stability is NOT governed by the clamp stiffness the pair represents:
         (0.05, 0.04) and (0.1, 0.08) have identical ts/tc, hence identical
         angular stiffness, yet the first fails 4/4 and the second is stable.
         It is set by how the constraint is discretised - tc in units of the
         1 ms step, and ts relative to solimp's default width of 0.001. The
         transmitted moment, by contrast, does follow ts/tc: steady pitch trim
         rises with it and saturates near 3 deg, where the rod's own bending
         stiffness takes over from the clamp.

         The result is still a clamp, not a pin: ~5 deg of pitch trim against
         ~0.5 deg for connect, so the clamp moment is genuinely transmitted.

         anchor is the far endpoint of the terminal capsule, size/(count-1) =
         0.1 m at count="13"; update it whenever count changes. relpose is
         deliberately omitted - MuJoCo's compiler fills it from qpos0, so the
         weld starts satisfied instead of snapping the two frames together.
         Swap weld -> connect for pinned ends (force only, no clamp moment). -->
    <weld name="noodle_left" body1="noodle/B_first" body2="elastic1/base_link"
          anchor="0 0 0" torquescale="0.1" solref="0.08 1"/>
    <weld name="noodle_right" body1="noodle/B_last" body2="elastic2/base_link"
          anchor="0.1 0 0" torquescale="0.1" solref="0.08 1"/>
  </equality>
</mujoco>
